        "title": "An astonishingly intricate project to restore a legendary theatrical dress",
        "subtitle": "Angela Wintle explains",
        "text": "On 28 December 1888, the curtain rose on a daring new production of Shakespeare's Macbeth at the Lyceum Theatre in London. Top of the bill, playing Lady Macbeth, one of the main characters in the play, was Ellen Terry, the greatest and best-loved English actress of her time. But she did not win this devotion through her acting ability alone. She understood the power of presentation and carefully cultivated her image, and that first night was no exception. When she walked on stage for the famous banquet scene, her appearance made the whole audience gasp.\n\nShe was wearing the most extraordinary clothes ever seen on a British stage: a long emerald and sea-green gown with tapering sleeves, under a velvet cloak, which glittered and sparkled strangely in the limelight. Yet this was no mere stage trick. The effect had been created with hundreds of wings from beetles. The gown, later named the 'Beetlewing dress', became one of the most iconic and celebrated costumes of the age.\n\nTerry was every bit as remarkable as her costumes. At 31 she became leading lady at the Lyceum Theatre, and for two decades she set about bringing culture to the masses. The productions she worked on were extravagant and daring. Shakespeare's plays were staged alongside blood-and-thunder melodramas, and their texts were ruthlessly cut. Some people criticised this, but they missed the point: the innovations sold tickets and brought new audiences to see masterpieces they would never otherwise have seen.\n\nHowever, it was a painter who made her immortal. John Singer Sargent was so struck by Terry's appearance at that first performance that he asked her to sit for him, and his famous portrait of 1889, now in the Tate Gallery in London, shows her with a glint in her eye, holding a crown over her flame-red hair. But while the painting remains almost as fresh as the day it was painted, the years have not been so kind to the dress. Its delicate structure, combined with the effects of time, has left it in an extremely fragile condition. So two years ago, Britain's National Trust launched a fundraising project to pay for its conservation.\n\nThe Trust turned to the textile conservator Zenzie Tinker to do the job. Zenzie loves historical dress because of its link with the past. 'Working on costumes like the Beetlewing dress gives you a real sense of the people who wore them; you can see the sweat stains and wear marks. But it's quite unusual to know who actually wore a garment. That's the thing that makes the Beetlewing project so special.'\n\nBefore any conservation work can begin, Zenzie and her team will carry out a thorough investigation to find out what changes have been made to the dress, and when. This will involve close examination of the dress for signs of damage and wear, helped by comparing it with John Singer Sargent's painting and with photographs from the time. Then Zenzie and the National Trust will decide how far back to take the reconstruction, as some members feel that even the most recent changes are now part of the history of the dress.\n\nThe first stages of the actual restoration will involve delicate surface cleaning with a small vacuum suction device. Once the level of reconstruction has been decided, the original crocheted overdress will be stitched onto a dyed net support before repairs begin. 'It's going to be extraordinarily difficult, because the original cloth is quite stretchy, so we've deliberately chosen net, because that has a certain amount of flexibility in it too,' says Zenzie. 'When the dress is displayed, none of our work will be noticeable, but we'll keep all the evidence on the reverse so that future experts will be able to see exactly what we've done, and I'll produce a detailed report.'\n\nZenzie estimates that the project, which will cost about £30,000, will need more than 700 hours of work. 'It will be a huge undertaking, and I don't think the Trust has ever spent quite as much on a costume before,' she says. 'But this dress is unique. It's very unusual to see this level of workmanship on a theatrical costume, and it must have looked spectacular on stage.' If Terry were alive today, she would no doubt be delighted. Unlike many other actresses, she valued her costumes: she kept them and reused them time and time again. 'I'd like to think she'd see our contribution as part of the ongoing history of the dress,' says Zenzie."
